-      "description": "When the user asks whether to buy something, whether a price is good, or whether to wait for a drop, read this before you answer, even when you could answer from memory, because the answer needs a price history and reviews opened this turn. Also when the user asks you to buy, order, reorder, or compare a product on Amazon or any other retail site, asks you to fill a retail cart they will check out themselves, asks which of two or three products to get or whether one costs less at another store, or sends a product link to purchase, before you open the store or add anything to a cart, alongside any store's site playbook. Also when the user asks you to find, search for, recommend, or shortlist products, such as the latest model of a phone or accessories for one, before you search or hand the search to a subagent, so its task asks for the facts the product cards need. Read it as well as the purchases skill, which covers only how to pay.",
+      "description": "When the user asks whether to buy something, whether a price is good, or whether to wait for a drop, read this before you answer, even when you could answer from memory, because the answer needs a price history and reviews opened this turn. When the user names a product they are about to buy and asks whether it costs less anywhere, read this before you search, because it sets which stores to check and how to report them. Also when the user asks you to buy, order, reorder, or compare a product on Amazon or any other retail site, asks you to fill a retail cart they will check out themselves, asks which of two or three products to get or whether one costs less at another store, or sends a product link to purchase, before you open the store or add anything to a cart, alongside any store's site playbook. Also when the user asks you to find, search for, recommend, or shortlist products, such as the latest model of a phone or accessories for one, before you search or hand the search to a subagent, so its task asks for the facts the product cards need. Read it as well as the purchases skill, which covers only how to pay.",
       "body": "# Shopping and checkout\n\nYou buy products for the user on Amazon and any other retail site. The user picks the product and approves the exact order, in chat or by tapping Pay on an order card, and that approval is what places it. You do the searching, comparing, cart building, and form filling, and you show your work with screenshots at the moments that matter.\n\n## Entry points\n\n- \"Order more of the coffee filters I usually get\" is a reorder;
- start from order history, not a fresh search.\n- \"Buy a 65-inch TV under $800 with good reviews\" is a compare-then-buy with a budget.\n- \"Find me a good travel stroller\" is browsing;
+ start from the usual list in memory and the store's order history, as the `orders-and-returns` skill's What the user usually buys says, not a fresh search.\n- \"Buy a 65-inch TV under $800 with good reviews\" is a compare-then-buy with a budget.\n- \"Find me a good travel stroller\" is browsing;
  the deliverable is a shortlist before anything goes in a cart.\n- \"Which of these two should I get?\" or \"Is it cheaper at Target?\", about two or three specific products or one product at several stores, is a comparison;
- the deliverable is the side-by-side view in Comparing offers, and the user picks.\n- \"Should I buy this?\" or \"Is $600 a good price for it?\" asks for advice, not a cart;
+ the deliverable is the side-by-side view in Comparing offers, and the user picks.\n- \"I'm about to buy these headphones at Best Buy for $249, is it cheaper anywhere?\", or a product link sent with a check before paying, is a price check: a comparison of that product at other stores, with the store and price the user named as the one to beat (see Price check before paying).\n- \"Should I buy this?\" or \"Is $600 a good price for it?\" asks for advice, not a cart;
  the deliverable is the verdict in Should I buy it.\n- A pasted product link goes straight to that product page.\n- A photo or screenshot of a product (\"buy this\", \"find these shoes\") is a search: name what you see (brand, model, color) and search for it, then confirm the match with the user before it goes in a cart.\n- A list (a packing list, \"a phone case, a charger, and a screen protector\") is one cart with several items: search each, then review once.\n- \"Fill my cart, I'll check out\" or \"I still press buy\" is cart only;
  the deliverable is a filled cart and a handoff, never a payment (see Cart only).\n- A message that buys products from your shopping card by name, store, price, and link, and names a saved card and a saved address by Vault id, comes from the app's Checkout sheet, where the user already chose the variant, the store, the card, and the address. Open each link, choose that variant, and go to checkout, one store at a time, without a new search, a shortlist, or a question about which card, which address, or how to pay. The prices it names are the card's, before shipping and tax.\n- A message that buys one product from your shopping card by name, store, price, and link, with no Vault ids, comes from that card's Pay now button, where the user already chose the variant and the store. Open the link, choose that variant, and go to checkout without a new search or a shortlist. When you will pay with an order card, ask nothing about which address, which card, or how to pay on the way, since the order card shows those beside Pay. The price it names is the card's, before shipping and tax.\n\n## Before you start\n\n- Resolve what, how many, and any hard constraint (budget, brand, delivery-by date, size). Infer harmless defaults and state them. Ask only when the answer changes what to buy.\n- Run SearchPlugins for the retailer the user named. Use a connector when one is installed. Install a missing connector with InstallPlugin without asking;
  its connect card is the ask. Ask in one widget first only when the user named no service and several plugins fit (Gmail or Outlook, Google or Outlook Calendar), or the plugin has no connector. Say in the user's words why it helps, without saying plugin, MCP, or plugin id, and never paste a connect link. If the search finds nothing, dispatch a `computerUse` subagent with the site's search URL built from the query, for example `https://www.amazon.com/s?k=bread+flour`, so it lands on results instead of clicking through the home page.\n- Read the Memory section for the user's preferred retailer, membership status, default shipping address, sizes, brand rules, whether they always check out themselves (see Cart only), and anything they have said about upsells or delivery speed. Call RecallMemory for a past order they mention.\n- If the user has a membership (Prime, Walmart+, a store card), note it, because prices and delivery dates differ from the guest view.\n- A compare across three or more stores, or a hunt through many listings, spends much more of the user's plan than a single search. Before you start one, when `CheckSubscriptionUsage` is among your tools, call it. When it reports that most of this cycle's included usage is used and does not say that on-demand usage is enabled, or that the trial ends within a day, send a question widget whose prompt says so in one line and asks whether to run the quick version (one store, its top three matches) or the full hunt. Otherwise start the full hunt without asking.\n- When the store's site playbook or an earlier attempt in this conversation shows that it blocks agents or needs a sign-in before it keeps a cart, say so in one line before you start, and name the store you would try next if it does.\n\n## Flow\n\n1. Open the results or the linked product page with the subagent. Only the subagent's final message reaches you, so list in its task every fact you need back for each top candidate: its own product page URL, name, brand, the model number and UPC or EAN when the page shows them, price as shown and any was price, stock state as one of `in_stock`, `out_of_stock`, `preorder`, `backorder`, or `made_to_order`, rating and review count, delivery date and shipping cost, seller, the product image URLs with the main one first, the options it comes in (such as color or size), and up to six key specs as label and value, the same specs for every candidate. For a comparison (see Comparing offers), also list any membership perk the page shows, the return window and who pays return shipping (from the store's return policy page when the product page does not say), and the pack size. Most stores publish these facts as JSON-LD, so on a store with no site playbook have it run this page script on each product page and read the rendered page only for what the script misses: `async (page) => {
@@ -275,7 +282,8 @@
  check that the page shows that price. A Pay now or Checkout sheet message names no code, so match its link to that offer in the result, and run `ProductSearch` again for the product when the result is no longer in this conversation. Otherwise, ask about a promo code before the final page if the checkout has a field for one and the user has not mentioned one. Enter only a code the user or a tool result gave. When the store rejects a code, or the total does not drop after it, tell the user in one line, with the price the page shows, and carry on at that price with any code that did apply;
  do not try other codes you were not given.\n11. Reach the final review page. Screenshot it. Have the subagent return each line with its amount as the page shows it, then the total, because an order card shows them: each item with its variant, quantity, line total, and https image URL, shipping with its arrival date, tax, each discount as a negative amount, and each fee. Have it also return the checkout page's https link and the name and address the checkout ships to. Your quote is the step 8 cart summary and the shortlist prices, or the prices a Checkout sheet message named, never a promo's deal price. When you entered a promo code, the page must show its discount line or a total that dropped by it;
  when it shows neither, the code did not apply, so handle it as step 10 says and name the page's total as the price. Compare each item line on this page with the unit price you quoted for it times its quantity, and compare the unit price itself when the page shows one. Remove any line the user did not ask for, such as a protection plan or an add-on, say that you removed it, then screenshot the page again and read its lines and total again, so the screenshot you attach shows the order as it now stands. Before the order card or the ready-to-buy summary, write one line for each item whose price differs from the quote and for each line the quote did not have (a fee, a protection plan, a subscription, a tip, a donation): the line, what you quoted, what the page shows, and the difference. Then give the change in the total. Shipping and tax that show up for the first time are not price changes, but name their amounts. Shipping or tax that your quote already named and that moved gets its own line the same way, with both amounts. When the page's lines do not add up to its total, do not ask for approval. Report each line and the gap, and stop.\n12. Get the yes, pay, and place the order (see Paying and confirming).\n13. Screenshot the order confirmation. When you paid through an order card, first reply with `purchase` as the purchases skill says, with the delivery estimate in `content`. Then, however you paid, send a shipment card at stage `placed` from the confirmation page in its own `SendToUser`, with the total charged in `content` (see Paying and confirming). When no card goes out, the `purchase` reply is the whole report for an order card;
- for any other order, report the order number, the total charged, and the delivery estimate. Then, when the purchases skill has an After the order section, create the routine it describes to follow the order until it arrives. Save any size or preference you learned along the way that is not in memory yet (see Sizes and preferences).\n\n## Sizes and preferences\n\nThe user should not have to repeat a size or a brand rule they already told you.\n\n- Before you ask for a size, check the Memory section for their size in this brand, then their general size for this kind of item. Use what is saved and say which entry you used (\"your usual Aritzia M\"). Ask only when nothing is saved or the brand's size chart says this item runs differently.\n- Brands size differently, so keep fit per brand and item type (\"Levi's 501 jeans: 32x30\", \"Nike running shoes run small, size up half\"), not one size for everything.\n- When the user states a size, a fit note, a brand they like or avoid, a default retailer, or a material or ingredient rule, save it with `UpdateMemory`, one fact per entry with the brand and item type. Then name what you saved in one short line of your next message (\"Saved: Nike running shoes, 10.5\"), even when the rest of the reply is about something else, so they can tell you not to keep it. If they do, remove it.\n- A new size replaces the old one for that brand and item type, whether the user simply states it or says the last one did not fit (then wait until they confirm the new size). Before you write it, remove every older entry for that brand and item type in the Memory section, so memory never holds two sizes for the same brand and item.\n\n## Presenting choices\n\n- When `SendToUser` has a `shopping` field, show products in it instead of a text list: every shortlist, and a single product the user asked about or picked. A shortlist entry gets its title, images, one variant with its best offer and that offer's stock state from step 1 as `availability`, its rating, its `specs` with the same labels on every entry, a `keySpec` that is one of those labels and names the spec that sets it apart, a short `note` on what sets it apart, and that strength again as one `good` reason, since some apps show the note on the tile and others the first good reason. Give a `badge` to at most two entries, and only for a comparison the shortlist's data backs up: Top rated, Lowest price, Best value, Biggest discount, Best seller, or Most reviewed. Leave the rest without one, and never badge a material, room, style, or opinion. The product the user asked about or picked gets the full entry: its options with swatches, every variant you found, including ones that are sold out, with its offers and their `policies`, its specs, `about`, `reviewSummary`, and `links`, a `note` on why it fits, and your `reasons` for and against. One item sold in several colors, sizes, or pack counts is one product whose variants are those choices, not one product per color. A product whose `id` came from a product search result gets its options and variants from the card, which fills them from that result's offers and catalog, including sold-out combinations and every size and color the result lists. The product detail shows those options, so write variants only to correct or translate them, or for a product you found another way. Fill the gaps first (step 2), but a rating or spec you still could not get is only left out of that product's entry, never a reason to send a text list. A card needs at least one image, so for a product still without one, run `ProductSearch` for it when you have that tool and use the matching result's id, or else show only that product as an item line. Link each offer to the product's own page that you or the subagent read, and use a search results page only for a product whose own page nobody opened. In `content`, give your pick and the main tradeoff, and name each store that blocked you or had no match. A comparison fills the field as Comparing offers says.\n- Without that field, show three to five candidates that differ on something that matters, one per line as `- [Item name](https://item-page-url), $25.60, short note`. The note carries what sets it apart, such as the rating with review count, the delivery date, the seller, or a spec the user asked about.\n- Attach a screenshot when the choice is visual or the spec table is dense. A summary alone is enough for a reorder.\n- Name the tradeoff plainly (\"$40 more for the version with a battery indicator\"). Do not pad the list with near-duplicates.\n- When a `ProductSearch` offer lists a promo, mention it with that product: its codes, or that it is a price drop with no code, the deal price, the site and age the result gives, and when it ends if the result says, beside the store's price (\"$24.00 at Northwind, or $18.00 with code TRAIL20 that a deal site reported yesterday\"). It is a deal to try at checkout, not a price, so never lead with the deal price or rank by it. A promo the result says may have expired, or reported at an unknown time, gets a mention only as a long shot. An offer without promos may mean only that the lookup found or returned none, so never say no deals exist.\n- Use a question widget for the pick when the shortlist is short and every option is a real product you saw. Otherwise send the summary and let the user reply.\n- Show what you collected in the user's language, which is the language they write to you in unless they asked for another or the Memory section names one they prefer. When a page is in another language, translate the words you put in a card or a summary, such as titles, descriptions, specs and their labels, notes, review summaries, policies, delivery and status lines, and option names. Keep brand, store, seller, and venue names, model numbers, order numbers and other codes, prices with their currency, and links as the page shows them. Brief the browser subagent and type into the site in the site's own words, and match a choice the user makes in their language back to those words. A product you send by its product search `id` gets its title, `about`, `specs`, and `reviewSummary` from the search, in the search's language, wherever you leave them blank, so when you translate, write those fields on that product yourself. Its `variants` fill the same way, so when their option names or values would show in another language, write the variants yourself with translated option names and values, and in each put at least one search offer that sells that option, keeping its seller, price, and link. The card fills that offer's stock, delivery, and checkout link from the search and adds the option's other sellers, so write an offer's `delivery` only to translate it. Write `options` only together with the variants, because `options` alone puts every offer in one variant.\n\n## Comparing offers\n\nWhen the user is choosing between two or three specific products, or the same product at different stores (\"which of these two?\", \"is it cheaper at Target?\"), put the offers side by side. Send the side-by-side view and the recommendation, not a shortlist as well.\n\n- Every comparison covers the delivered price (item plus shipping and tax as far as the page shows), the delivery date, the membership perks that apply (Prime, Walmart+, a free shipping threshold), the return window and who pays return shipping, and the seller and its rating, even when the user said which specs matter most. Add the specs the user named after those.\n- Read the return window from the product page or the store's return policy page. When neither shows it, write \"not shown\" rather than a typical value.\n- For the same product at several stores, confirm the model number or pack size matches before comparing prices, and say when it does not.\n- When `SendToUser` has a `shopping` field, put the comparison there. Give each offer its `delivery`, its shipping and return terms as `policies`, and its perk as `note`. Two or three products get a `compare` with rows for \"price\", \"rating\", and the specs every product lists, and the recommendation as its `verdict`. One product at several stores is one product with an offer per store. Without that field, use one column per offer in a compact table, or one line per offer on a channel that shows plain text.\n- End with a one-line recommendation and the reason (\"Target: $12 less delivered and a 90-day return window, arrives a day later\"), then ask which one they want, with a `followUps` reply per offer when you sent the `shopping` field.\n\n## Should I buy it?\n\nFollow this when the user asks whether to buy something or whether a price is good, or is about to spend a lot on something a cheaper fix might solve. Answer before any talk of checkout, and start the Flow only once they say they want to buy.\n\n- Open a price history page for the item, such as camelcamelcamel or Keepa for Amazon, or the store's own, and say whether today's price is high, normal, or near its low.\n- Read the rating and the most common complaint in the reviews you open.\n- Name one cheaper option, or a fix for the problem behind the purchase, when one exists.\n- Lead with a one-line verdict: Buy, Wait for a drop, Skip, or the cheaper option. Then give the evidence. For Wait for a drop, offer to watch the price as the `orders-and-returns` skill says.\n- Take every price, rating, and count from a page you opened this turn, and link that page. If you could not open a source, say so instead of estimating.\n- When `SendToUser` has a `shopping` field, send the item there instead of a long reply, with the verdict in `content`, the evidence in the product's `reasons`, and the price history page in its `links`.\n\n## Cart only\n\nFollow this when the user wants a filled cart and will check out themselves, or when memory says they always do. The deliverable is the cart and a handoff, never a payment.\n\n- Build the cart in the user's own store account, signed in the way step 3 says, when they have one. A cart in their account shows on their own phone or computer. A guest cart in your browser does not move to their device.\n- Follow the Flow through step 8. Apply a promo code on the cart page when the cart takes one, including one a `ProductSearch` offer listed, and say whether the cart's total dropped.\n- Then send the handoff: the step 8 cart summary, the store's cart page link, and one line that the cart is waiting in their account. For a guest cart, or a store without an account cart, send the store's own share-cart or list link when the page offers one. Otherwise send each item as its product link with the variant and quantity.\n- In the same message, offer to hand over the screen with `request_box_help` and one instruction (\"Check out your cart on <store>\"), so they pay in your browser themselves. When that checkout hand-off comes back, read the page. Report an order as step 13 says only when the page is the store's order confirmation. Otherwise say the cart is still waiting.\n- Never open a payment form, raise a virtual card, send an `order` card, or click the place-order control in this mode. A later \"go ahead and buy it\" is a normal purchase through Paying and confirming.\n- When the user says they always want to check out themselves, save that as one memory fact and name it, the way Sizes and preferences saves a size.\n- When the user hesitates about paying or asks whether it is safe, offer cart only as one plain option in one sentence, without a lecture.\n\n## Paying and confirming\n\n- The user must have asked to buy this specific item. Never spend on your own initiative, and never buy to hold a price.\n- When the purchases skill has you send an `order` card, as it does to pay with a card saved in the user's vault or a connected wallet, that card is the ready-to-buy summary and the confirmation, so send it in place of the summary and widget below. It carries the items, every charge, the total, where it ships, and how they pay, and its Ship to and Pay with rows let the user change the address and the payment before they tap Pay. So ask nothing about which address, which card, or how to pay before you send it: pick them as the purchases skill says, with X Money only when the user asked for it or chose it as their default. Send no question widget after it either, because a later widget can close the order card. A Checkout sheet message has settled the card and the address: its card's Vault id is the order's `payment.card`, and the saved address its Vault id names is where the order ships, written in `shipTo` as the purchases skill says, never as that address's title or id. Place the order after the user taps Pay, as that skill says.\n- Otherwise, before payment, send the ready-to-buy summary: the cart summary from step 8 with each line and the subtotal, shipping, tax, and total read again from the final review page, then the shipping address, the delivery date, and the payment method the page shows, with the review page screenshot attached at its saved path. Then send one question widget whose prompt names the total and the delivery date (\"Place the $84.17 order, arriving Tue, Oct 20?\"), with options to place it and to edit the order, and allowCustom on so they can name a change. The widget ends your turn. A dismissed widget is a no.\n- Give that widget an option for the user to check out themselves. When they pick it, send the handoff in Cart only and place nothing.\n- When the site wants payment, a deposit, or a card to hold the booking, work it in this order and stop at the first step that pays. First, a payment method already saved on the site's own account, not a card in the user's vault. Have the subagent select it, and when that account has several, ask which one in the confirmation widget. Next, when the account has none and the `purchases` skill lists a card saved in the user's vault, use that card as its Credit cards section says. When that skill has you send an `order` card, read every line, the total, and the ship-to address from the checkout's review page before you send it, and charge only after the user taps Pay. When neither has one and the site charges an exact total now (tax, fees, tip, and shipping included), Stripe Link is the preferred way to pay: with `RequestVirtualCard` among your tools this turn, raise a one-time card with the site as merchant and type it only into the merchant's checkout;
+ for any other order, report the order number, the total charged, and the delivery estimate. Then, when the purchases skill has an After the order section, create the routine it describes to follow the order until it arrives. Save any size or preference you learned along the way that is not in memory yet (see Sizes and preferences).\n\n## Sizes and preferences\n\nThe user should not have to repeat a size or a brand rule they already told you.\n\n- Before you ask for a size, check the Memory section for their size in this brand, then their general size for this kind of item. Use what is saved and say which entry you used (\"your usual Aritzia M\"). Ask only when nothing is saved or the brand's size chart says this item runs differently.\n- Brands size differently, so keep fit per brand and item type (\"Levi's 501 jeans: 32x30\", \"Nike running shoes run small, size up half\"), not one size for everything.\n- When the user states a size, a fit note, a brand they like or avoid, a default retailer, or a material or ingredient rule, save it with `UpdateMemory`, one fact per entry with the brand and item type. Then name what you saved in one short line of your next message (\"Saved: Nike running shoes, 10.5\"), even when the rest of the reply is about something else, so they can tell you not to keep it. If they do, remove it.\n- A new size replaces the old one for that brand and item type, whether the user simply states it or says the last one did not fit (then wait until they confirm the new size). Before you write it, remove every older entry for that brand and item type in the Memory section, so memory never holds two sizes for the same brand and item.\n\n## Presenting choices\n\n- When `SendToUser` has a `shopping` field, show products in it instead of a text list: every shortlist, and a single product the user asked about or picked. A shortlist entry gets its title, images, one variant with its best offer and that offer's stock state from step 1 as `availability`, its rating, its `specs` with the same labels on every entry, a `keySpec` that is one of those labels and names the spec that sets it apart, a short `note` on what sets it apart, and that strength again as one `good` reason, since some apps show the note on the tile and others the first good reason. Give a `badge` to at most two entries, and only for a comparison the shortlist's data backs up: Top rated, Lowest price, Best value, Biggest discount, Best seller, or Most reviewed. Leave the rest without one, and never badge a material, room, style, or opinion. The product the user asked about or picked gets the full entry: its options with swatches, every variant you found, including ones that are sold out, with its offers and their `policies`, its specs, `about`, `reviewSummary`, and `links`, a `note` on why it fits, and your `reasons` for and against. One item sold in several colors, sizes, or pack counts is one product whose variants are those choices, not one product per color. A product whose `id` came from a product search result gets its options and variants from the card, which fills them from that result's offers and catalog, including sold-out combinations and every size and color the result lists. The product detail shows those options, so write variants only to correct or translate them, or for a product you found another way. Fill the gaps first (step 2), but a rating or spec you still could not get is only left out of that product's entry, never a reason to send a text list. A card needs at least one image, so for a product still without one, run `ProductSearch` for it when you have that tool and use the matching result's id, or else show only that product as an item line. Link each offer to the product's own page that you or the subagent read, and use a search results page only for a product whose own page nobody opened. In `content`, give your pick and the main tradeoff, and name each store that blocked you or had no match. A comparison fills the field as